**Design.** The two classes differ only in a label and message wording. Put the computation in an abstract base that implements `Bank`, and let subclasses supply the strings (Template Method).

```java
interface Bank {
    void addLoans(int[] loans);
    double averageLoans(); int maxLoans(); int minLoans();
}

abstract class LoanDept implements Bank {
    protected int[] loans;
    private final int capacity;
    LoanDept(int capacity) { this.capacity = capacity; this.loans = new int[capacity]; }

    protected abstract String completionMsg();
    protected abstract String averageMsg(double avg);

    public void addLoans(int[] in) {
        loans = Arrays.copyOf(in, Math.min(in.length, capacity)); // ignore overflow
        System.out.println(completionMsg());
    }
    public double averageLoans() {
        double avg = Arrays.stream(loans).average().orElse(0);
        System.out.println(averageMsg(avg));
        return avg;
    }
    public int maxLoans() { return Arrays.stream(loans).max().orElse(0); }
    public int minLoans() { return Arrays.stream(loans).min().orElse(0); }
}

class PersonalLoanDept extends LoanDept {
    PersonalLoanDept(int clients) { super(clients); }
    protected String completionMsg() { return "clients processing completed"; }
    protected String averageMsg(double a) {
        return String.format("Average of loan amount amongst clients %.2f", a);
    }
}
```
`BusinessLoanDept` mirrors it with its own strings. Copy the exact strings from the statement, including its spelling; the max and min messages follow the same pattern. State the overflow reading you chose ("keep at most capacity elements").

**SOLID and DRY.** One place for math (DRY), subclasses only vary messages (open for extension), callers depend on `Bank` (dependency inversion). Possible improvement: pass the labels into one class by composition if the only difference is strings.

**Valid Parenthesis String (greedy).** Track `lo` and `hi`, the minimum and maximum possible number of unmatched `(`. For `(` both increase; for `)` both decrease; for `*` do `lo--` and `hi++`. Clamp `lo` at 0; if `hi < 0` at any point return false. At the end return `lo == 0`. O(n) time, O(1) space. A DP or two-stack solution also works but uses more memory.
